	// ListSecurityGroupAttachments List what holds a security group
	//
	// **Visibility:** `internal` · `public` (x-visibility)
	//
	//
	// Every live resource this security group is attached to — VPCs, clusters and instance groups — in one list.
	// This is the inverse of `GET .../networking/security-groups-effective`. That operation asks what filters an OBJECT; this one asks what holds a GROUP. Both are needed because a binding is stored on the holder (`Cluster.securityGroups`, `InstanceGroup.securityGroups`, `VPC.securityGroups`), so the group's own resource cannot report who points at it, and there is no way to derive one direction from the other.
	// **For a tenant-defined group, it is the `DELETE` conflict, readable.** Such a group, while still attached, cannot be deleted: the `DELETE` is refused with `409` and `details[].reason: CONFLICT_IN_USE`, naming every holder in one prose message. This operation returns that same set, resolved by the same lookup, as structured entries — so a client can show what is blocking a delete before attempting one, and link to each holder to detach it. Those holders are detachable: a VPC with `POST .../networking/vpcs/{id}`, a cluster with `PATCH .../compute/clusters/{id}`, an instance group with `PATCH .../compute/instance-groups/{id}`, each sending its `securityGroups` without this group.
	// **A platform-managed VPC default group is the exception, in both halves.** Its `DELETE` is refused with `409` and `details[].reason: CONFLICT_IMMUTABLE` — never `CONFLICT_IN_USE` — before holders are ever consulted, so it names none and this list is not "what the `DELETE` would say". This operation still reports the owning VPC, which is the truthful answer to "what holds this group" and is worth reading: it tells you which VPC the group belongs to. But that VPC is not a holder you can detach to make the group deletable. The group is deletable only with its VPC. To change a VPC's posture, attach your own group, which displaces the default from the binding.
