// CreateWebhookSubscriptionWithBodyWithResponse Create a webhook subscription
//
// **Visibility:** `public` (x-visibility)
//
// **Who may call this:** Any authenticated caller, in their own partition.
// Any member of a customer organization may write that organization's
// subscriptions (no org-admin role is needed); in the provider partition
// only platform-admin may, and other provider personnel are refused with
// 403. A customer with no resolvable organization is refused with 401.
//
// Subscribe a customer endpoint to notification events. A signing secret is
// generated when not supplied and returned ONCE in the response (store it — it
// is never returned again). Dispatch requires the event-intake fan-out relay to
// be enabled (NOTIFICATIONS_RELAY_ENABLED, on by default) — when it is disabled
// a subscription is still stored and signed but no callbacks are sent.
//
// A subscription belongs to the caller's partition: a customer's to its
// organization, and provider personnel's to the provider partition, which
// receives every platform-wide event, security events included. Creating a
// provider subscription therefore requires the platform-admin role (403
// otherwise); a customer needs no platform role to subscribe its own
// organization.
//
// ## Verifying a delivery
//
// Every delivery is signed with HMAC-SHA256 under the subscription's signing
// secret. Verification is the only thing distinguishing a real delivery from a
// forged one, because the subscriber URL is reachable by anyone who learns it.
//
// Each delivery carries these headers:
//
// | Header | Value |
// | --- | --- |
// | `X-K0r-Signature` | `t=<unix>,v1=<hex>` — the send timestamp and one or more signatures (see below) |
// | `X-K0r-Signature-Version` | The subscription's current `secret_version`. Informational; not needed to verify |
// | `X-K0r-Event-Id` | CloudEvents `id` of the delivered event |
// | `X-K0r-Event-Source` | CloudEvents `source` of the delivered event |
//
// ### The signature header
//
// `X-K0r-Signature` is a comma-separated list:
//
// ```text
// X-K0r-Signature: t=1756200000,v1=3c6c68833522137223525ca5735879a84495ac0b0ef5f30ce0dd35d6858c9463
// ```
//
//   - `t` — the Unix timestamp, in seconds, at which this request was sent. It is
//     stamped per attempt, so a retry carries its own `t`, not the original one.
//   - `v1` — HMAC-SHA256, lowercase hex, over the bytes `<t>` + `.` + the raw
//     request body. `v1` identifies the **signing scheme**, not the secret: it
//     means "HMAC-SHA256 over `<t>.<body>`, lowercase hex".
//
// There may be **more than one `v1` entry**. During a signing-secret rotation
// the delivery is signed under both the new and the previous secret so that a
// subscriber can redeploy on its own schedule — see "Rotating a signing secret"
// below. Accept the delivery if your secret reproduces **any** entry. Do not
// assume a fixed number of entries, do not assume an order, and do not try to
// work out which entry belongs to which secret — you hold one secret and only
// need to know whether it matches. Ignore any element whose key you do not
// recognise, so that a future scheme version does not break your verifier.
//
// ### To verify
//
//  1. Parse `X-K0r-Signature` into `t` and the list of `v1` values.
//  2. Reject the delivery if `t` differs from your own clock by more than
//     **300 seconds** in either direction. See "Replay protection" below.
//  3. Read the **raw request body bytes**, before any JSON parsing.
//  4. Compute `HMAC-SHA256(signing_secret, "<t>." + raw_body)` — the timestamp as
//     received, a literal `.`, then the body bytes unchanged.
//  5. Compare your hex digest against each `v1` value using a constant-time
//     comparison. Accept on the first match; reject if none match.
//
// Verify against the bytes as received: parsing and re-serialising the JSON can
// reorder keys or alter whitespace, producing a different signature.
//
// Worked example:
//
// ```python
// import hmac, hashlib, time
//
// TOLERANCE_SECONDS = 300
//
// def verify(secret: str, raw_body: bytes, header: str) -> bool:
//
//	timestamp = None
//	signatures = []
//	for element in header.split(","):
//	    key, _, value = element.partition("=")
//	    if key == "t":
//	        timestamp = int(value)
//	    elif key == "v1":
//	        signatures.append(value)
//	if timestamp is None or not signatures:
//	    return False
//
//	# Reject a stale or future-dated delivery before doing any crypto.
//	if abs(time.time() - timestamp) > TOLERANCE_SECONDS:
//	    return False
//
//	signed = f"{timestamp}.".encode() + raw_body
//	expected = hmac.new(secret.encode(), signed, hashlib.sha256).hexdigest()
//	return any(hmac.compare_digest(expected, s) for s in signatures)
//
// ```
//
// ## Replay protection
//
// The subscriber URL is reachable by anyone who learns it, so a delivery
// captured off the wire could otherwise be resent indefinitely. The timestamp
// is **inside the signed bytes**, which is what makes the freshness check
// meaningful: an attacker replaying a captured request cannot move `t` forward
// without invalidating every `v1` entry, and cannot recompute the entries
// without the secret.
//
// The tolerance is **300 seconds** and is enforced **by you**, not by the
// platform — we cannot assess the freshness of our own outbound request. The
// number is published so every integrator uses the same one.
//
// 300 seconds is safe against the retry schedule because each attempt is signed
// at the moment it is sent. A delivery retried an hour later carries an
// hour-later `t` and is fresh on arrival; cumulative retry backoff never pushes
// a legitimate delivery outside the window. Pair the freshness check with
// deduplication on (`X-K0r-Event-Source`, `X-K0r-Event-Id`) rather than relying
// on either alone.
//
// ## Rotating a signing secret
//
// `rotateWebhookSubscriptionSecret` issues a new signing secret without
// recreating the subscription. The previous secret keeps working for a fixed
// **24-hour** overlap window, during which every delivery carries a `v1` entry
// under each secret. That window is what makes rotation usable: your receiver
// holds one secret in its configuration, and you cannot swap that configuration
// in the same instant you call the API. While your fleet is part-way through a
// rollout, instances on either secret verify successfully.
//
// A rotation therefore needs no coordination with us. Call rotate, store the
// new secret, deploy it at your own pace, and confirm from
// `previous_secret_expires_at` how long you have left.
//
// `secret_version` increments on each rotation and is echoed on every delivery
// as `X-K0r-Signature-Version`, so you can see whether your receivers have
// caught up. It plays no part in verification.
//
// Two edges worth knowing:
//
//   - Rotation is **not** a revoke. The previous secret stays valid for the full
//     window. If a secret has leaked and you need it dead immediately, rotate with
//     `{"overlap": false}` — the previous secret is discarded rather than parked
//     and stops verifying at once, at the cost of breaking any receiver that has
//     not yet been updated.
//   - Rotating again while a window is still open replaces the parked secret with
//     the one being rotated out. Only the most recent previous secret is retained,
//     so the earlier one stops working immediately even if its
//     `previous_secret_expires_at` has not yet passed.
//
// ## How your response is interpreted
//
// Not every failure is retried, and the difference matters:
//
// | Your response | What happens |
// | --- | --- |
// | `2xx` | Acknowledged; the delivery is settled |
// | `4xx` other than `429` | **Permanent failure — dead-lettered immediately, never retried** |
// | `429`, any `5xx`, timeout, connection error | Retried |
//
// A `4xx` is read as "this subscriber will never accept this event", so the event
// is dropped for good on the first attempt. If your endpoint can fail transiently,
// return `429` or a `5xx` for the retryable case — returning `400` from a
// temporarily broken handler loses the event permanently.
//
// This interacts with signature verification, so decide deliberately what your
// receiver returns when a signature does not match. A blanket `400` on a
// verification failure means a delivery signed under a secret your fleet has not
// finished deploying is dead-lettered on the first attempt rather than retried.
//
// ## Retries and duplicates
//
// A retryable failure is retried with a 5ⁿ-second backoff capped at five minutes,
// until the configured attempt limit, after which it is dead-lettered.
//
// Each attempt is signed at the moment it is sent, so a retry carries its own
// timestamp and its own signatures — the raw body is unchanged, but
// `X-K0r-Signature` differs between attempts. Do not cache or compare signature
// headers across attempts, and do not treat a changed signature as evidence of
// tampering.
//
// Retries reuse the same event, so a subscriber can receive the same delivery more
// than once and must be idempotent. Deduplicate on the (`X-K0r-Event-Source`,
// `X-K0r-Event-Id`) pair, which is the server's own uniqueness key for a delivery
// and is stable across retries. A duplicate is also possible after a `2xx`: if the
// server fails to record the acknowledgement, the delivery is re-sent.
//
// ## Payload contract and versioning
//
// The webhook body (`NotificationEvent`) is a public contract, and it evolves
// under one rule: a new field MAY be added at any time, and a receiver must
// ignore fields it does not recognise. Changing the meaning of an existing field
// or removing one is a breaking change and will only be made through a versioned
// change with a published migration period — never silently.
//
// The `data` of an event delivered to an organization's endpoint holds only
// the fields its event type defines for customers; a field the producing
// service sent outside that list is never delivered there. The provider's own
// subscriptions receive all of `data`.
//
// Build your receiver to tolerate additive growth, and keep that tolerance
// separate from the two mechanisms above, which are NOT affected by new fields:
//
//   - **Validate** only the fields you understand, and ignore the rest — a field
//     you have never seen is expected, not an error.
//   - **Deduplicate** on the `X-K0r-Event-Source` / `X-K0r-Event-Id` headers (see
//     "Retries and duplicates"), never on the body's field set.
//   - **Verify the signature** over the entire raw body exactly as received,
//     including fields you do not recognise (see "Verifying a delivery"). Do not
//     strip unknown fields before verifying — that changes the bytes and breaks the
//     signature.
//
// ## Network requirements
//
// Your endpoint must be reachable from the platform's outbound (egress) network,
// and deliveries arrive from the platform's egress addresses — not from a fixed
// address embedded in this API. Those source addresses are a property of the
// deployment you are integrating with, not of the contract, so if your receiver
// is behind an allow-list or firewall, get the current egress ranges to permit
// from your platform operator's networking/deployment guide rather than assuming
// a value here. There is no inbound connection from you to the platform for
// delivery — it is one-way, platform → the URL you registered over HTTP(S) (a
// plaintext `http://` receiver on an internal network is supported; see the `url`
// field below).
//
// Takes any type of body and a specified content type, and returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with POST /v1/regions/global/notifications/webhooks (the `CreateWebhookSubscription` operationId).
func (c *ClientWithResponses) CreateWebhookSubscriptionWithBodyWithResponse(ctx context.Context, contentType string, body io.Reader, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*CreateWebhookSubscriptionResponse, error) {
	rsp, err := c.CreateWebhookSubscriptionWithBody(ctx, contentType, body, reqEditors...)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return ParseCreateWebhookSubscriptionResponse(rsp)
}

