// RotateWebhookSubscriptionSecretWithBodyWithResponse Rotate a webhook subscription's signing secret
//
// **Visibility:** `public` (x-visibility)
//
// **Who may call this:** Any authenticated caller, in their own partition.
// Any member of a customer organization may write that organization's
// subscriptions (no org-admin role is needed); in the provider partition
// only platform-admin may, and other provider personnel are refused with
// 403. A customer with no resolvable organization is refused with 401.
//
// Issue a new signing secret for an existing webhook subscription. The
// subscription id, URL and event-type filter are unchanged, and no delivery is
// dropped — unlike deleting and recreating the subscription, which loses
// everything queued in between.
//
// The new secret is returned **once**, in this response, and cannot be
// retrieved again.
//
// Rotating a provider subscription requires the platform-admin role, the same
// rule as creating one.
//
// ## The overlap window
//
// The previous secret keeps working for a fixed **24 hours** after rotation.
// Throughout that window every delivery carries a signature under **both**
// secrets, as two `v1` entries in `X-K0r-Signature`, so a receiver holding
// either one verifies successfully.
//
// That is the point of the operation. Your receiver holds one secret in its
// configuration and you cannot swap that configuration in the same instant you
// call this endpoint; while a fleet is part-way through a rollout, instances on
// either secret keep working. Store the new secret, deploy it at your own pace,
// and read `previous_secret_expires_at` for your deadline.
//
// The window is 24 hours in every deployment and is not configurable.
//
// `secret_version` increments on each rotation and is echoed on every delivery
// as `X-K0r-Signature-Version`, so you can tell whether your receivers have
// caught up. It takes no part in verification, and it does not label the `v1`
// entries — a delivery sent mid-overlap reports one version and carries two
// signatures.
//
// See "Verifying a delivery" on `createWebhookSubscription` for the procedure
// itself.
//
// ## Rotation is not a revoke
//
// The previous secret stays valid for the full window, so rotating does not
// immediately stop a leaked secret from producing valid signatures.
//
// Send `{"overlap": false}` when you need that: the previous secret is
// discarded rather than parked and stops verifying at once, and
// `previous_secret_expires_at` comes back null. This is the right choice for a
// suspected leak and the wrong one for routine rotation — any receiver that has
// not already been updated starts rejecting deliveries immediately, which the
// delivery worker sees as `4xx` and dead-letters.
//
// ## Rotating twice inside a window
//
// Only the most recent previous secret is retained. Rotating again while a
// window is still open replaces the parked secret with the one being rotated
// out, so the earlier secret stops working immediately even if its
// `previous_secret_expires_at` has not yet passed.
//
// This is deliberate: refusing to rotate while a window is open would lock you
// out of rotating for 24 hours, which is precisely the wrong behaviour during an
// incident.
//
// Takes any type of body and a specified content type, and returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with POST /v1/regions/global/notifications/webhooks/{id}/rotate (the `RotateWebhookSubscriptionSecret` operationId).
