// DeleteProjectInOrgWithResponse Delete project
//
// **Visibility:** `internal` · `public` (x-visibility)
//
// Delete a project and tear down its dedicated Kubernetes namespace.
//
// Asynchronous, and a 204 does NOT mean the project is gone. The project moves
// to `status: deleting` and stays visible to GET and LIST while its namespace
// is removed on the management cluster and on every configured region. It
// disappears only once the teardown reports completion, so poll
// `GET .../projects/{id}` and treat the first 404 as the success signal. The
// namespace may still be terminating after that: objects inside it holding
// finalizers can keep it there.
//
// While a project is `deleting` its identifier stays reserved and it accepts no
// new work — a cluster create in it is rejected with 409. Both matter.
// Releasing the identifier at acceptance would let a new project take the name
// while the old teardown was still running, and that teardown would then delete
// the new project's namespace; allowing new clusters would let a create
// re-create the namespace after the teardown removed it, leaving the namespace
// behind with no project to own it.
//
// Tear down the project's clusters first. A delete is refused with 409 while
// the project still holds clusters, because removing the namespace would remove
// every object inside it — including the ClusterDeployment resources backing
// those clusters — without their normal teardown running. CAPI would never
// reconcile them, leaving the clusters listed by the compute API with their node
// reservations still held while their Kubernetes objects were gone. This check
// is best-effort: it reads the quota records the platform already keeps, so it
// cannot see a cluster created while quota enforcement was unconfigured, or one
// admitted under the quota fail-open override before the reconcile sweep caught
// up.
//
// Two exceptions to the contract above, both on deployments that cannot
// schedule a teardown at all — no workflow integration configured, or an
// identifier too long to have ever named a namespace. There the project is
// deleted outright and 204 means what it says, because no completion report
// will ever arrive. The service logs an Error naming the namespace it left
// behind, and reports the disabled integration at startup.
//
// Once the delete finalizes, the project's id becomes available for reuse:
// creating a project with the same id afterwards succeeds. It is not available
// before then, so wait for the first 404 rather than retrying a create. Before
// the id is released, the project's project-scoped service accounts are
// disabled, its IAM role bindings are purged, and its quota limits are
// removed, so a re-created project does not inherit them. That is the order
// the service uses, and the first two are deliberately in that sequence.
//
// Disabling the service accounts is a side effect worth planning for: a
// service account scoped to this project stops authenticating once the delete
// finalizes, because its roles are held on the account itself rather than as
// bindings, and would otherwise pass to a project re-created with this id.
// Provision a new service account against the new project rather than reviving
// the old one.
//
// Reviving one is possible and is not safe: the disable leaves the account's
// roles in place, and setting its status back to `active` restores them
// against whatever project now holds this id, with no check that the id still
// names the same project. Anyone with permission to update service accounts in
// the organization can do it, so treat these accounts as retired rather than
// as suspended.
//
// Project-scoped API keys are NOT disabled at all, so treat any that named
// this project as still live and revoke them explicitly.
//
// Granting
// a member's roles through this API is refused with 409 while the project is
// `deleting`, which narrows the window in which a grant can slip in behind the
// purge without closing it: the refusal is decided before the grant is
// written, so a grant already in flight can still land after the purge, and on
// the two immediate-delete deployments described above there is no `deleting`
// window for it to refuse in at all. A binding created directly through the
// IAM policy API is not covered either, because IAM has no notion of a
// project's lifecycle. Treat "wait for the 404, then grant" as the rule rather
// than relying on the refusal.
//
// The binding purge and the quota clear run as part of the step that releases
// the id, so if they cannot complete, the project keeps its id. On the normal path the platform retries
// it automatically, so a failure there is self-healing; if the failure lands
// after the bindings were purged, the members are locked out until that retry
// converges. On the two deployments described above, where the delete completes
// immediately because no teardown could be scheduled, nothing retries it, so
// repeat the DELETE; there the same lockout lasts until a human does, because
// the role bindings can have been purged before the write that was supposed to
// finish the delete failed. Retrying the DELETE completes it; keeping the
// project instead means re-granting its bindings.
//
// Two limits apply to a re-created project. First, its predecessor's resources
// in other services (networks, subnets, VPCs and the like) key the project id
// and are NOT removed by this call, so they remain addressable under the new
// project; the 409-while-it-holds-clusters check above reduces the cluster part
// of that exposure, and only that part: it counts clusters, so resources of
// every other kind are not covered by it, and even for clusters it is a
// best-effort check rather than a proof. Its own caveat above says why it
// cannot see a cluster created while quota enforcement was unconfigured or
// admitted under the fail-open override, and a ledger read that fails lets the
// delete through as well. Second, quota consumption is accounted per
// organization rather than per project, so deleting a project does not free
// quota; the resources it held must themselves be deleted.
//
// Returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
