// ListSecurityGroupsEffectiveWithResponse Get the effective security groups of an object
//
// **Visibility:** `internal` · `public` (x-visibility)
//
// Report what actually filters an object's machines: the object's own security-group binding merged with the floor of each VPC it sits in, with every group's rules, grouped by VPC.
// A security group can be attached to a cluster, to an instance group, and to a VPC. `Cluster.securityGroups` and `InstanceGroup.securityGroups` report only the first of those and `VPC.securityGroups` only the second, both as bare ids. The platform merges them — the owner's groups first, then the VPC's floor, a group in both applied once — when it renders the fabric. This operation is that merged answer, so a client does not have to reproduce the merge from two id lists and then issue one `GET` per group to see the rules.
// Address the object with `objectKind` + `objectId`, both required. A `cluster` or `instance_group` returns one block per VPC it materialized; a `vpc` returns a single block with that VPC's own binding only — the owner's groups also filter traffic on interfaces inside it, and are read from the owner.
// **Read one block, not the union.** An interface belongs to exactly one VPC, so the block for that VPC is the whole filter set for a machine attached through it. Concatenating the blocks would overstate what any one machine sees when an owner has more than one VPC.
