// DeleteSecurityGroupWithResponse Delete security group
//
// **Visibility:** `internal` · `public` (x-visibility)
//
// Delete a security group and the rules it carries.
// Teardown is asynchronous: the operation returns `204` immediately and the group flips to `state: deleting`. Poll `GET .../networking/security-groups/{id}` until it returns `404` to confirm the rules are no longer in force. There is no job resource and no `202`.
// A group mid-teardown does NOT release its `id`, so a create reusing that `id` is refused with `409` until the group is gone. A repeated `DELETE` on a group that is already `deleting` is an idempotent `204`.
// A group that is still ATTACHED to a resource cannot be deleted: the request is refused with `409` naming everything that still holds it — VPCs, clusters and instance groups together, in one message — and the group is left untouched.
// Every holder is detachable, so a `409` is always resolvable without deleting anything:
